# Part 4 Notes — Parameter Sensitivity Experiments

## 1. Changes or improvements from previous part

- Reused the geometry-based graph from Part 3 and added systematic parameter sweeps.
- Tested how rescue changes under different diffusion strengths, decay rates, corrected fractions, and rescue thresholds.
- Shifted the project from model construction toward hypothesis testing and sensitivity analysis.

## 2. Key issues with model

- Rescue still depends on an instantaneous threshold rather than sustained biological exposure.
- Parameter values remain exploratory and are not fitted to experimental measurements.
- The geometry is still simplified, so conclusions should be interpreted as mechanism-testing rather than biological prediction.

## 3. Thoughts for next step forward

- Introduce cumulative exposure so rescue depends on signal integrated over time.
- Compare instantaneous rescue against exposure-based rescue to see how the biological interpretation changes.
- Use the next part to make the rescue rule more biologically plausible while keeping the deterministic model interpretable.

